  "query_preview": "..\n\nA compact mid level short wave along the nose of an upper jet streak \nis now exiting to the southeast of the forecast area, taking \nlingering showers and scattered thunderstorms with it. Potent mid \nlevel subsidence in its wake will expand across Se Mi during the \ncourse of the morning, reflected in model soundings depiction of a \ndeepening subsidence inversion. Sfc dewpoints are shown to remain in \nthe 60s into the afternoon. This will sustain enough low level \nmoisture to support a fairly healthy stratus/strato cu field today. \nThere are at least some indications in NAM and HRRR soundings that \nsome low stratus development in the morning may lend to some \nsprinkles or areas of light drizzle before diurnal boundary layer \ngrowth lifts cloud bases. Slight cool air advection within northerly \nflow and the cloud cover will warrant highs from the mid 70s to lower\n80s today.\n\nA push of low level dry air from the northwest will then commence \nthis evening and persist into Wednesday as high pressure begins to \nexpand into the region from the west. This will be in response to \nbuilding heights across the upper midwest and Great Lakes region. \nMid level anticyclonic flow and sfc high pressure will sustain dry \nconditions into Thursday. The drier air will promote a little better\nmixing Wed and Thurs, with the opportunity for decent radiational \ncooling, thus leading to greater diurnal spread in temperatures.\n\nDeterministic model solutions show reasonable agreement in \nforecasting a deepening of a slow moving mid level wave from the \nupper Midwest into the Mid Mississippi/Lower Ohio Valley Friday into \nthis weekend. The extent to which corresponding mid level troughing, \ndeep layer moisture and diurnal instability impact Se Mi carries a \nlittle more variability among model ensemble members. Probabilistic \nguidance has at least trended a little higher with rain chances \nFriday night and Saturday, warranting the chance of \nshowers/thunderstorms in the forecast.",
